WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.251 --> 00:00:08.755
So 40 percent of the world’s population
live near the coast.

00:00:08.797 --> 00:00:12.467
So you’ve got sea level rise,
intense storms

00:00:12.509 --> 00:00:14.386
and, by some estimates,

00:00:14.427 --> 00:00:17.764
half a billion to a billion people
will be adversely affected

00:00:17.806 --> 00:00:20.725
by what's going to happen in the coast.

00:00:22.102 --> 00:00:24.062
But that's not the only problem.

00:00:24.062 --> 00:00:25.981
It's a bit more complicated than that.

00:00:25.981 --> 00:00:27.774
It's not just storms are increasing,

00:00:27.774 --> 00:00:31.194
sea level rise
is going to flood these areas.

00:00:31.194 --> 00:00:33.029
You have secondary issues,

00:00:33.029 --> 00:00:37.117
such as acidification
that's going to damage coral reefs

00:00:37.117 --> 00:00:38.660
and natural systems.

00:00:39.619 --> 00:00:44.541
You have changing wind patterns
because ocean temperatures are increasing

00:00:45.834 --> 00:00:49.045
and storms are getting more intense
and also more unpredictable.

00:00:49.963 --> 00:00:54.134
And on top of that,
you've got increased rainfall

00:00:54.175 --> 00:00:58.138
and flooding that is happening
deeper in the coast

00:00:58.179 --> 00:00:59.848
in coastal communities.

00:00:59.889 --> 00:01:01.182
This is a big problem.

00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:04.853
And it's a personal problem
for me as well.

00:01:04.894 --> 00:01:08.940
I'm from Singapore,
an island state in Southeast Asia.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:10.817
Many coastal communities.

00:01:11.776 --> 00:01:14.946
I worry about what's going
to happen to my region,

00:01:14.946 --> 00:01:16.656
how it's going to impact my family.

00:01:16.656 --> 00:01:20.035
I've got three boys that, you know,
have grown up along the coast.

00:01:21.161 --> 00:01:25.206
And with that,
I've spent the last two years

00:01:25.248 --> 00:01:31.004
helping countries and coastal communities
build resilience against climate change.

00:01:31.838 --> 00:01:33.423
What's the challenge?

00:01:33.465 --> 00:01:37.677
The challenge is no two coasts are alike.

00:01:37.719 --> 00:01:40.513
They are highly diverse,
physically diverse.

00:01:40.555 --> 00:01:43.892
They have different
distributions of populations.

00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:46.686
And they are socioeconomically different

00:01:46.728 --> 00:01:49.314
in terms of the communities
that live there.

00:01:49.314 --> 00:01:52.275
Now, there are many solutions
to protect the coast.

00:01:53.026 --> 00:01:56.613
You have seawalls, storm barriers.

00:01:56.613 --> 00:01:59.324
We can restore mangroves.

00:01:59.324 --> 00:02:00.867
And this can protect the coast.

00:02:00.867 --> 00:02:02.952
The challenge is not
so much the solutions.

00:02:02.952 --> 00:02:05.872
The challenge is actually
what's the right mix of solutions

00:02:05.914 --> 00:02:07.582
that fits the local context

00:02:07.624 --> 00:02:10.251
and delivers the highest
socioeconomic return.

00:02:11.002 --> 00:02:14.964
Now, let me illustrate this
with an example.

00:02:16.091 --> 00:02:21.262
I worked with a government
in Southeast Asia,

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:28.937
a country with a large population,
diverse set of islands,

00:02:28.978 --> 00:02:31.690
many coastal communities, spread across.

00:02:32.816 --> 00:02:38.530
And one region in particular
faced a complex interplay of challenges.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:41.449
Rising sea levels that were
causing permanent flooding.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:45.412
Increasing storms
that were damaging the coast.

00:02:45.412 --> 00:02:51.167
A large proportion of vulnerable people,
in poverty, living in low-lying areas.

00:02:51.209 --> 00:02:54.003
On top of that, land subsidence
had made matters worse.

00:02:54.921 --> 00:03:00.093
So we worked with the government
to model the impact of climate change

00:03:00.135 --> 00:03:01.511
over the coming decades

00:03:01.553 --> 00:03:03.555
to answer questions like:

00:03:03.596 --> 00:03:06.057
Which parts of the coast will be flooded?

00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:07.976
Which populations will be impacted?

00:03:08.852 --> 00:03:13.898
Where would schools, hospitals,
critical infrastructure be damaged?

00:03:13.898 --> 00:03:17.402
How would this impact crops,
water systems?

00:03:18.528 --> 00:03:20.780
What would be the impact
on natural ecosystems

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:22.949
that are important to this country.

00:03:25.285 --> 00:03:30.206
And importantly, what this did
was that it combined climate science

00:03:30.248 --> 00:03:32.250
with data analytics,

00:03:32.292 --> 00:03:33.668
with local context,

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:35.837
and linked it to socioeconomic impact.

00:03:37.088 --> 00:03:40.508
Now, to give an example,
one particular community faced the risk

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:43.511
of 100,000 people
potentially being displaced

00:03:43.553 --> 00:03:46.181
because of increasing sea levels.

00:03:46.222 --> 00:03:48.141
Eight percent of the economy

00:03:48.183 --> 00:03:52.145
and almost half
of the region's wetland reserves

00:03:52.187 --> 00:03:55.607
were at risk of being damaged.

00:03:56.149 --> 00:03:58.485
Now, in addition, with this insight

00:03:58.485 --> 00:04:02.113
the government was able
to better understand the knock-on effects

00:04:02.113 --> 00:04:06.743
that this would have
on public health, on food security,

00:04:06.743 --> 00:04:08.578
on forced migration.

00:04:08.578 --> 00:04:10.497
So a lot of issues.

00:04:11.623 --> 00:04:13.041
So what do you do about that?

00:04:13.082 --> 00:04:18.129
I mean, this massive exposure
and no clear answers in what you do,

00:04:18.171 --> 00:04:20.423
and in a developing economy such as this,

00:04:20.465 --> 00:04:24.511
massive trade-offs that you need to manage
in terms of where you deploy resources.

00:04:24.552 --> 00:04:26.304
So ...

00:04:26.346 --> 00:04:30.391
Initially this region
was looking at building seawalls,

00:04:30.433 --> 00:04:33.144
having early warning systems,
storm barriers in place

00:04:33.186 --> 00:04:36.940
that required a fair bit
of capital and investment.

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:41.778
In fact, what we found was
that a mix of solutions were needed.

00:04:41.778 --> 00:04:46.658
So that included mangrove restoration
in selected areas as natural defense

00:04:46.658 --> 00:04:49.536
and only in certain parts of the coast

00:04:49.536 --> 00:04:52.789
investing to build
storm barriers and seawalls.

00:04:53.456 --> 00:04:56.668
Now with that,
the region could consider --

00:04:58.294 --> 00:05:02.006
with the same amount of resources,
maximizing lives saved,

00:05:02.048 --> 00:05:05.051
livelihoods protected
and ecosystem safeguarded.

00:05:06.052 --> 00:05:09.889
And on top of this, the cost of inaction,
of not doing anything,

00:05:09.931 --> 00:05:14.394
also meant massive negative impact
for the private sector.

00:05:14.435 --> 00:05:18.690
So private enterprises would have had
billions of dollars of losses

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:21.985
with manufacturing,
with critical infrastructure,

00:05:21.985 --> 00:05:24.279
with commercial real estate being damaged.

00:05:24.279 --> 00:05:26.906
Now, having a visibility on this,

00:05:26.906 --> 00:05:29.826
the government could
then engage the private sector

00:05:29.826 --> 00:05:33.329
on codeveloping and cofunding solutions

00:05:33.329 --> 00:05:36.165
that benefitted both private enterprise

00:05:36.207 --> 00:05:37.542
and the local community.

00:05:38.459 --> 00:05:42.422
Now, this is not a problem
only in Southeast Asia.

00:05:42.463 --> 00:05:46.301
A city in West Africa, for example,
had similar challenges

00:05:46.342 --> 00:05:50.263
but compounded with very dense
populations along the coast,

00:05:50.305 --> 00:05:51.848
severe land subsidence

00:05:52.932 --> 00:05:55.018
and drainage challenges

00:05:55.059 --> 00:05:59.606
that were exacerbated
by poor solid waste management

00:05:59.606 --> 00:06:02.191
in some parts of the city.

00:06:02.191 --> 00:06:08.031
Now by integrating climate science
data analytics to local context

00:06:08.031 --> 00:06:11.159
and tightly linking this
to socioeconomic impact,

00:06:11.159 --> 00:06:14.245
the government was able
to manage the trade-offs

00:06:14.245 --> 00:06:18.333
on how you would balance
the deployment of resources

00:06:18.374 --> 00:06:20.126
to things such as:

00:06:20.168 --> 00:06:22.337
Do I build more drainage infrastructure?

00:06:22.378 --> 00:06:25.173
Do I relocate some populations
to other parts?

00:06:25.214 --> 00:06:27.425
Do I build storm barriers?

00:06:27.467 --> 00:06:29.052
What's the right mix?

00:06:30.094 --> 00:06:34.057
Now as you can see,
this is a challenging and complex problem.

00:06:34.891 --> 00:06:37.310
And when you look at it,

00:06:37.352 --> 00:06:41.397
the challenge is every coast
that you look at is different.

00:06:41.397 --> 00:06:44.734
Right? They're socioeconomically,
physically different.

00:06:44.734 --> 00:06:50.281
And the most pressing challenges
are often in developing countries

00:06:50.281 --> 00:06:53.660
where they have limited capacity to adapt

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:55.536
and they have big trade-offs to manage.

00:06:56.579 --> 00:06:59.707
On top of this, there are
many technical solutions

00:06:59.749 --> 00:07:01.376
and natural solutions out there.

00:07:01.417 --> 00:07:05.088
You can protect the coast
with mangrove restoration,

00:07:05.129 --> 00:07:06.965
seawalls, storm barriers.

00:07:07.006 --> 00:07:10.218
You can accommodate
by flood-proofing buildings,

00:07:10.259 --> 00:07:11.886
building drainage.

00:07:11.928 --> 00:07:15.765
You can relocate people
and critical infrastructure.

00:07:15.807 --> 00:07:18.351
The challenge is not so much
what are these solutions,

00:07:18.393 --> 00:07:23.189
rather what's the right mix of solutions
for any given context.

00:07:23.982 --> 00:07:27.610
So by combining climate science,
data analytics,

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:31.364
to local considerations,
tightly to socioeconomic outcomes,

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:35.284
decision makers can
really balance the trade-offs

00:07:35.284 --> 00:07:37.912
and take the right actions over time

00:07:37.912 --> 00:07:40.873
to be able to maximize lives saved,

00:07:40.915 --> 00:07:44.794
livelihoods protected
and natural ecosystems saved.

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:46.921
So with that ...

00:07:48.047 --> 00:07:50.591
we could protect more people
along the coast

00:07:50.633 --> 00:07:52.802
and optimize the outcomes.

00:07:53.469 --> 00:07:54.721
Thank you.

00:07:54.762 --> 00:07:56.931
(Applause)

